<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Armstrong Custer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Armstrong Custer was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army officer who rose to fame as a cavalry commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. After the war he led the 7th Cavalry on the Great Plains, and his expedition of 1874 confirmed the presence of gold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>. He and over 260 of his men were killed by Lakota and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> warriors at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-little-bighorn">Battle of the Little Bighorn</a> in June 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer.jpg" alt="George Armstrong Custer" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer is Mathew Brady (died 1896) and his field staff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Custer_Bvt_MG_Geo_A_1865_LC-BH831-365-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George Armstrong Custer was born in New Rumley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, on 5 December 1839, the son of Emanuel Henry Custer, a farmer and blacksmith, and Marie Ward Kirkpatrick. According to family letters he was named after a minister, George Armstrong, in his devout mother's hope that he might join the clergy. He graduated from the United States Military Academy at West Point, last in his class. On 9 February 1864 he married Elizabeth Clift Bacon, known as Libbie, whose father had at first disapproved of a blacksmith's son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Commissioned a second lieutenant, Custer served at the First Battle of Bull Run on 21 July 1861. In June 1863 the cavalry commander Alfred Pleasonton, looking for aggressive officers, promoted him to brigadier general of volunteers at the age of 23, commanding the Michigan Cavalry Brigade. He fought at Gettysburg, where his brigade defeated J. E. B. Stuart's attack at East Cavalry Field. In 1864 he served under Philip Sheridan in the Overland Campaign and in the Shenandoah Valley, defeating Jubal Early at Cedar Creek, and in April 1865 his division blocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a>'s retreat and received the first Confederate flag of truce.</p><p>After the war he commanded cavalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, and in July 1866 he became lieutenant colonel of the newly created 7th Cavalry Regiment at Fort Riley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>. By the time of his Black Hills Expedition of 1874, tension between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the Lakota and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> was very high. The United States wanted to take possession of the Black Hills and its gold deposits, and in 1875 the Grant administration tried to buy the region. When the Sioux refused, they were ordered to report to reservations by 31 January 1876, a deadline that winter conditions made impossible to meet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the spring of 1876 Custer was to command one of three columns, marching west from Fort Abraham Lincoln. On 25 June 1876, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-little-bighorn">Battle of the Little Bighorn</a> in the Montana Territory, he was killed together with every soldier of the five companies he led. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> warriors did not know Custer was in the fight until weeks later. The assignment of blame continues: Major Marcus Reno's flight to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and Captain Frederick Benteen's late arrival have both been cited.</p><p>His widow Libbie published several books about him and did much to build what is called the Custer myth, which the secrecy of the 1879 Court of Inquiry record, released only in 1951, also supported. The reaction to his life remains divided.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Armstrong Custer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 7th Cavalry under <b>George Armstrong Custer</b> attacked a Cheyenne village on the Washita River, killing Black Kettle and many women and children.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-27">27 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After gold was found in the Black Hills in 1874, the Great Sioux War followed; Lakota and Cheyenne under Sitting Bull and Crazy Horse defeated <b>Custer</b> at the Little Bighorn in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-sioux-nation">Great Sioux Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in New Rumley, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Big Horn County, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.4,-81.03&amp;year=1863" aria-label="New Rumley on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2560 720 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George Armstrong Custer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3010 990h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3010 990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.4,-81.03&amp;year=1863">What was here in 1863? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
